The maintainer chose premise (b) for the first traverse of a 3+2
plane: the X/Y is unknown, but the tool starts at the tool-change
height, the top of the Z travel. The upright law uses the same premise.
table_read now takes the worst start over the X/Y travel at the table
top plus the Z stroke (GenJob::z_stroke, from the mounted Z axis). The
table counts only when the move along the tool axis from there can
end under the table top. The verdict names the lowest plane that
every such move clears: h_max - stroke / axis_z. The past-90-deg note
stays (#3081) and now names the tool-change height. A machine with no
finite Z stroke is read with stroke 0, the stricter reading.

The multiaxis/indexed catalog Tilted pocket (A30 B30, plane 52.120)
is clear: from Z500 the move ends at Z-124.5. The catalog case does not
change. NOT covered: a program that starts with the tool low near the
table (after a manual jog); the decision file says so.

